    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The sūtra teaches that the many Buddhist paths are skilful means within a single One Vehicle that leads all beings to Buddhahood.</li><li>It presents the Buddha's lifespan as immeasurable: his death is described as a teaching device, and he is said to remain active in the world.</li><li>Little known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, it became the main scripture of the Tiantai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tendai">Tendai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nichiren">Nichiren</a> schools and shaped the art, poetry and devotion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In the Japanese poetry compendium Kanwa taisho myoho renge-kyo, drawn from more than 120 collections of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-period">Heian period</a>, over 1360 poems refer to the Lotus Sūtra in their titles alone.</p></aside>
    </section>

